<BaseLayout title="Scientific ML & Bayesian Inference — Jonas El Gammal" description="Gaussian-process surrogate models and active learning for expensive Bayesian inference.">
  <div class="shell"><header class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">Surrogate inference for expensive likelihoods</span><h1>Scientific ML & Bayesian inference</h1><p>Gaussian-process surrogate models and active learning for Bayesian inference when every call to the likelihood matters.</p></header></div>
  <article class="prose">
    <h2>The computational bottleneck</h2><p>Bayesian inference can become prohibitive when each likelihood evaluation calls a cosmological solver, waveform model, or slow simulation. Conventional Monte Carlo methods may require far more evaluations than the available budget permits.</p>
    <figure class="feature-gif"><img src="/assets/img/gpry_animation.webp" alt="GPry building a surrogate posterior through active learning"/><figcaption>The GPry loop: evaluate, learn a surrogate, acquire the next informative locations, and test convergence.</figcaption></figure>
    <h2>GPry</h2><p>I develop <a href="/gpry/"><strong>GPry</strong></a>, an open-source framework that learns a Gaussian-process surrogate of the log-posterior. An active-learning loop chooses evaluations where they are most useful, while explicit diagnostics decide when the reconstruction is sufficiently stable.</p><p>The surrogate is paired with a classifier that learns which parts of parameter space are uninteresting or invalid. This is important in scientific applications, where a solver may fail for some parameters and the posterior can span a very large dynamic range. NORA, GPry’s batch-acquisition method, uses Monte Carlo samples from the surrogate to propose several informative evaluations at once rather than repeatedly optimizing a multimodal acquisition function.</p>
    <h2>Selected papers</h2><ul class="paper-list"><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.02045"><strong>Fast and Robust Bayesian Inference using Gaussian Processes with GPry</strong></a><span>Core method · JCAP 2023 · arXiv:2211.02045</span><p>Introduces GPry and demonstrates reductions of roughly two orders of magnitude in expensive posterior evaluations.</p></li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19267"><strong>Parallelized Acquisition for Active Learning using Monte Carlo Sampling</strong></a><span>NORA batch acquisition · arXiv:2305.19267</span><p>Uses Monte Carlo samples from the surrogate to construct robust, parallel batches of new evaluations.</p></li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.21871"><strong>Accelerating LISA inference with Gaussian processes</strong></a><span>LISA application · Physical Review D 2025 · arXiv:2503.21871</span><p>Compares GPry with nessai on three simulated LISA sources and quantifies both posterior accuracy and computational cost.</p></li></ul>
    <h2>Where it works—and where it doesn’t</h2><p>GPry is most useful for deterministic, sufficiently smooth, low- to moderate-dimensional targets where likelihood evaluations dominate runtime. It is not a universal replacement for MCMC or nested sampling, and difficult separated modes or very high dimensionality require care.</p>
  </article>
